    "created_at": "2019-09-08T19:45:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-09-08T19:45:48Z",
    "body": "> Can't you just ask the operating system for the time through Rust?\r\n\r\nThere are two problems with this right now: one solveable with a little work, one not so easily solved.\r\n\r\nThe easier problem is that starship is intentionally stateless at the moment (it's a fresh process launch for every prompt draw). This could be changed e.g. with some caching mechanism to record the last time the prompt was drawn, but we're not quite there yet.\r\n\r\nThe bigger problem is that without shell support, we only know how long it took between prompt draws--we have no idea what portion of that time was spent running a command and what portion was spent idling.\r\n\r\nFor example, if I hit ENTER, then wait 5 seconds, then spend 2 seconds typing in a command, and it takes 3 seconds to run, there were 10 seconds between prompt draws, but only 3 of those were spent executing the command. Without support from the shell (firing a hook to start the timer when the command is run), there is no way to distinguish this from a command that I instantly typed and executed, but took 10s to run.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/starship/starship/issues/comments/529235303/reactions",
